SLIDE 6 — Ride Optimization: Goal and Context
⏱ ~2.5 minutes
The goal here is specific and measurable: determine the optimal suspension parameters for a 6×2 vehicle equipped with a Non-Reactive Suspension — or NRS — configuration, in order to minimize cabin seat RMS acceleration.
Why does this matter? In heavy commercial vehicles, the driver is exposed to continuous road-induced vibrations throughout an entire shift — often 8 to 10 hours at a stretch. These vibrations propagate through the suspension, into the chassis, into the cabin, and ultimately reach the driver's seat.
RMS acceleration at the cabin seat is the standard engineering metric for quantifying this. It is directly linked to driver fatigue, physical discomfort, and — over time — serious long-term health impacts. This is not an abstract performance metric; it has direct implications for driver wellbeing and, by extension, fleet safety and productivity.
The suspension system is the primary lever available to the engineer to control this. The question is: given the complexity of the system — multiple axles, coupled dynamics, multiple degrees of freedom — how do you find the optimal configuration efficiently?
That is what this work addresses.

SLIDE 7 — System Definition (Part 1)
⏱ ~2 minutes
The vehicle under study is a 6×2 configuration — meaning two rear drive axles and one front steer axle. The Non-Reactive Suspension system mechanically couples the rear tandem axles, which allows load sharing across both axles without introducing additional pitch or roll moments.
This is important because it means the system maintains vehicle attitude stability — the vehicle doesn't pitch forward or roll excessively when one axle encounters a bump — while still distributing the load effectively.
From a dynamics standpoint, we are modeling the sprung mass — which is the cabin and chassis — with three degrees of freedom: bounce, pitch, and roll. Each axle also has its own bounce and roll dynamics. This gives us a coupled, multi-degree-of-freedom system.

SLIDE 8 — System Definition (Part 2) / Tunable Parameters
⏱ ~2 minutes
The system is formulated using axle displacements, with the primary output we care about being the cabin seat-level RMS acceleration.
To optimize ride comfort, we tune four key parameters:
K_F — Front axle spring stiffness
C_F — Front axle damping coefficient
K_2 — Rear axle 1 spring stiffness
K_3 — Rear axle 2 spring stiffness
These parameters define how the suspension responds to road inputs. Finding the right combination — the one that minimizes seat-level vibration — is the core optimization problem.

SLIDE 9 — Two Approaches
⏱ ~3 minutes
To solve this, we developed and compared two approaches.
The first is the Physics-Based Approach. The workflow is: define the suspension ODE system, simulate road input by solving the ODEs, extract RMS acceleration from the result, and then optimize stiffness and damping using that result as the objective function.
The strengths are significant — it is physically reliable, grounded in first-principles mechanics, requires no training data, and produces fully interpretable results. However, the critical limitation is compute time. Each full optimization run takes 10 to 11 hours. Any change to road class, vehicle speed, parameter bounds, or damper model requires a complete re-run. For a team iterating through multiple design variants, this becomes a serious bottleneck.
The second approach is the Deep Learning Surrogate model. Here, we first generate training data from ODE simulations, train a deep learning model to replicate the system's response, and then use that trained model as a fast proxy inside the optimizer. Once trained, the surrogate predicts RMS acceleration in milliseconds — enabling hundreds of optimization iterations at negligible cost.
The trade-off: there is an upfront cost in generating the training data and training the model, and the accuracy is bounded by the quality of that training data.
These two approaches are not competing — they are complementary, as you'll see.

SLIDE 10 — Physics-Based Approach: ODEs
⏱ ~2 minutes
The physics-based model is built on a system of Ordinary Differential Equations that describe the dynamics of the suspension system — how the sprung and unsprung masses respond to road inputs over time.
These equations encode the physical relationships between spring forces, damping forces, mass inertia, and road excitation. Solving them gives us the time-history of accelerations at the cabin seat, from which we compute the RMS value.
This is the ground truth of the system. Everything we build on top of it — the surrogate model, the optimization framework — is ultimately validated against these equations.

SLIDE 11 — Physics-Based Approach: KKT and Baumgarte Stabilization
⏱ ~2.5 minutes
The ODEs represent a 6-degree-of-freedom suspension model with two geometric constraints — constraints that represent the mechanical coupling between axles in the NRS setup.
To enforce these constraints, we use Lagrange multipliers through a KKT formulation — the Karush-Kuhn-Tucker conditions. These multipliers introduce constraint forces into the equations of motion, ensuring the mathematical solution respects the physical boundaries of the system.
However, numerical integration of constrained differential equations introduces a known problem: constraint drift — where numerical errors accumulate over time and the constraint is gradually violated.
We address this using Baumgarte Stabilization — a technique that adds a correction term to the equations, effectively acting as a critically damped spring that continuously pulls any constraint violation back toward zero. The stabilization parameters control how quickly this correction is applied.
This is what makes the ODE simulation numerically robust over long simulation windows.

SLIDE 12 — Shock Absorber Design: Two Options
⏱ ~2.5 minutes
Real-world heavy commercial vehicle dampers are not linear — they have asymmetric, nonlinear characteristics. The damping force in compression is different from the damping force in rebound. This is by design — it gives engineers control over how the suspension handles different road events.
We explored two design strategies for incorporating this nonlinear behavior into the optimization.
Option 1 — Direct Optimization: The nonlinear damper model is embedded directly inside the ODE framework. The optimizer simultaneously tunes the linear parameters — K_F, C_F, K_2, K_3 — along with the nonlinear damper parameters: compression damping, asymmetry ratio, and compression and rebound factors. Everything is optimized together.
Option 2 — Two-Phase Optimization with Curve Fitting: In Phase 1, we optimize only the linear parameters. In Phase 2, we use the velocity time history from Phase 1 to fit the nonlinear damper parameters using a weighted least-squares curve fitting approach. The two phases are sequential.
Both options were tested. Let's look at what the results showed.

