Since both approaches produce equivalent results, we recommend Option 1 — Direct Optimization as the preferred pipeline.
Here is the reasoning: embedding the nonlinear damper directly into the ODE means the optimizer is always working on the true system representation. There is no post-processing step, no approximation introduced by a secondary curve fit. The physical consistency is preserved throughout the entire optimization loop.
Option 2 introduces an approximation in Phase 2 — fitting a nonlinear curve after the fact introduces a potential source of error that is absent in Option 1. When performance is equivalent, the cleaner and more rigorous approach is the right choice.

SLIDE 15 — Optimization: Single vs Multi-Objective
⏱ ~2 minutes
The optimization framework supports both single-objective and multi-objective formulations.
The cabin seat RMS acceleration is computed from three components: bounce acceleration, roll acceleration, and pitch acceleration — all evaluated at the seat level.
In single-objective optimization, these three components are aggregated into a single scalar metric, and the optimizer minimizes it directly.
In multi-objective optimization, each degree of freedom is treated as a separate objective. This produces a Pareto front — a set of optimal trade-off solutions where improving one objective comes at the cost of another. The engineer can then choose the solution that best fits their design priorities.
Both formulations are available within the framework, and the choice can be embedded into the workflow based on the specific design requirements.

