⏱ ~2 minutes
Moving to test evaluation — the surrogate model, trained on the current simulation dataset, successfully converged to an optimised suspension configuration.
The key point here: this same optimization that takes 10 to 11 hours using the physics-based approach runs in minutes using the trained surrogate. Same quality of answer, dramatically different compute time.
The framework is deployment-ready. The current training data is entirely simulation-derived. Replacing or augmenting it with real measured road and vehicle response data would further improve prediction accuracy and make the optimized parameters directly reflective of real operating conditions — which is the natural next step in production deployment.

SLIDE 23 — Generalised Framework
⏱ ~2 minutes
This brings us to the generalized framework.
What we have built is not a one-off solution for ride optimization. It is a flexible, reusable engineering optimization framework with four key properties.
It integrates both the ODE-based and surrogate-based approaches within a single framework, giving the engineer the choice of which modelling strategy to use based on their current needs.
It supports seamless switching between use cases — the same framework can be applied to any subsystem optimization problem where a physical model and an objective function can be defined.
It supports a range of optimization algorithms — single-objective, multi-objective, gradient-based, evolutionary — making it adaptable to different problem structures.
And it facilitates direct result comparison across methods, which supports better decision-making and model validation.

SLIDE 24 — Fitting into the Agentic Architecture
⏱ ~3 minutes
Now, let's close the loop. How does all of this — the ODE solver, the surrogate model, the optimizer — fit into the multi-agent architecture we described at the beginning?
The answer is: the physics code, the solver, and the optimizer are tools that the agent calls. They do not decide whether results make sense, or what to do when something goes wrong. That is the agent's responsibility.
At every stage of the workflow, the agent follows a decision framework with four possible paths:
Proceed — the stage completed cleanly, move forward. Retry — a recoverable failure occurred, reattempt before deciding whether to continue. Warn — the stage completed but the result is outside expected physical bounds, flag it and continue with a note. Stop — the failure makes continuing meaningless, halt and return a structured explanation to the user and to downstream agents.
Every decision is logged — which path was taken, and why. This audit trail is part of the structured output available to downstream agents and to the engineer reviewing results.
The five-step workflow embedded in the agent is:
Parse and Validate — physics code checked for syntax and model validity
Load Road Data — input road profile loaded and verified
Pre-flight Test — stability check and compute-time estimate
Run Optimiser — multi-objective Pareto or single-objective
Generate Outputs — results packaged into a structured summary
The physics never changes run to run. What the agent adds is the reasoning layer above it — deciding whether tools are working correctly, whether results are trustworthy, and ensuring nothing downstream receives incomplete or misleading information.

