APPENDIX: ANTICIPATED QUESTIONS & SUGGESTED ANSWERS
Q: How do you validate the surrogate model against real vehicle data? A: The current surrogate is trained on ODE simulation data. Validation against real data is the next phase — instrumented vehicle tests would provide ground-truth acceleration measurements that can both validate the ODE model and be fed directly into surrogate training.
Q: How does the central orchestrator handle conflicts between domain agents? A: The orchestrator maintains a priority hierarchy and a conflict resolution protocol. Domain agents surface conflicting outputs as warnings; the orchestrator logs the conflict and either flags it for human review or resolves it using predefined precedence rules depending on severity.
Q: Can this framework be applied to non-vehicle engineering domains? A: Yes. The N → N-1 → N-2 hierarchy and the agent pattern are domain-agnostic. The domain-specific knowledge is encoded in the individual agents, not in the architecture itself. The framework has been designed with that generalization in mind.
Q: What happens when the agent encounters a physics violation in the surrogate output? A: The physics-informed loss during training reduces the likelihood of this, but at inference time, the agent compares the surrogate output against expected physical bounds defined in the validation layer. An out-of-bounds result triggers the "Warn" or "Stop" path depending on the magnitude of the violation.
